= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <difference of two cubes> identity gives the real <polynomial factorization>
$$
\boxed{a^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).}
$$
Expanding the right side cancels the two mixed terms of each degree. The quadratic factor satisfies $a^2+ab+b^2=(a+b/2)^2+3b^2/4$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For real cube roots, set $u=a^{1/3}$ and $v=b^{1/3}$, so $(ab)^{1/3}=uv$. The <sum of two cubes> identity gives
$$
(u+v)(u^2-uv+v^2)=u^3+v^3=\boxed{a+b}.
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

<Completing the square> under the square root gives $\sqrt{(x-1)^2}=|x-1|$. Thus the equation is $|x-1|=2$, and the definition of <absolute value> gives $x-1=\pm2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{x=-1\quad\text{or}\quad x=3.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The domain excludes $x=0$. Rearranging the <inequality> gives
$$
\frac{x+1}{x}\geq0.
$$
A sign check on the <intervals> separated by $-1$ and $0$ shows that the ratio is nonnegative when $x\leq-1$ or $x>0$. The point $-1$ is allowed because it makes the numerator zero. Therefore
$$
\boxed{x\in(-\infty,-1]\cup(0,\infty).}
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The original PDF puts both $x+y$ in the exponent in the second equation. Since the <exponential function> $3^t$ is injective, $3^{x+y}=1$ gives $x+y=0$. Put $u=3^x>0$, so $3^y=1/u$. The first equation then gives
$$
u+\frac1u=2,\qquad (u-1)^2=0.
$$
Thus $u=1$, so $x=0$ and $y=-x=0$. The unique real solution of the <simultaneous equations> is
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(0,0).}
$$

