= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Pair adjacent terms in the finite <sum>:
$$
(-2+4)+(-6+8)+\cdots+(-98+100).
$$
There are $25$ pairs, each equal to $2$. The result is $\boxed{50}$.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $r=\sin(\pi/4)=1/\sqrt2$. The <geometric series> has first term $r$ and common ratio $-r$, with <absolute value> less than one. Therefore its <sum> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{r}{1+r}=\frac1{\sqrt2+1}=\sqrt2-1.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

The <product rule> gives
$$
y'=e^{-x}(3x^2-x^3)=x^2e^{-x}(3-x).
$$
For $x>0$, the <derivative> is positive before $3$ and negative after $3$. Also $y(0)=0$ and $x^3e^{-x}\to0$ as $x\to\infty$. Thus the global maximum on the requested range is
$$
\boxed{x=3,\qquad y_{\max}=27e^{-3}.}
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

For $x>0$, the denominator is positive, so the <function graph> has the same sign as $\sin x$. Its crossings of the horizontal axis are
$$
\boxed{x=n\pi,\qquad n=1,2,3,\ldots.}
$$
Each crossing is genuine because the <derivative> there is $(-1)^n/(n\pi)^2\ne0$. Near zero, $\sin x\sim x$, so $y\sim1/x\to+\infty$; the origin is excluded and is not a crossing. The oscillations alternate sign and decay towards zero as $x\to\infty$, with <absolute value> at most $1/x^2$.

\Image[paper-1-q7-oscillation.png]
{title=The positive-axis graph in Question 7, with its crossings at positive integer multiples of $\pi$}

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Use the <substitution rule> with $u=\sqrt x$, for which $du=dx/(2\sqrt x)$ on $x>0$. The <integral> becomes $\int e^u\,du$, giving the <antiderivative>
$$
\boxed{e^{\sqrt x}+C.}
$$

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

<Completing the square> gives
$$
(x-2)^2+y^2=1.
$$
Thus the <circle> has
$$
\boxed{\text{centre}=(2,0),\qquad\text{radius}=1.}
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

The <dot product> is
$$
\boxed{(a,a+1)\cdot(a+1,a)=2a(a+1)=2a^2+2a.}
$$
Both <vectors> have squared <Euclidean norm> $a^2+(a+1)^2=2a^2+2a+1$. Under the given condition $a^2+a=1/2$, their <dot product> is $1$ and each squared <Euclidean norm> is $2$. Hence
$$
\cos\theta=\frac1{\sqrt2\sqrt2}=\frac12,\qquad\boxed{\theta=\pi/3=60^\circ.}
$$

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the full set of <complex logarithm> values of $i$:
$$
\log i=i\left(\frac\pi2+2\pi k\right),\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.
$$
By the definition of <complex exponentiation>, the inner power can therefore take any value $i^i=\exp[-(\pi/2+2\pi k)]$, a positive real number. Its <complex logarithm> values are $-(\pi/2+2\pi k)+2\pi im$, where $m\in\mathbb Z$. Thus
$$
(i^i)^i=\exp\left[-i\left(\frac\pi2+2\pi k\right)-2\pi m\right]=-ie^{-2\pi m}.
$$
All possible <real parts> and <imaginary parts> are consequently
$$
\boxed{\Re((i^i)^i)=0,\qquad\Im((i^i)^i)=-e^{-2\pi m},\quad m\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Choosing the principal <complex logarithm> at both stages gives the particular value $-i$. The independent logarithm choices are needed because the question asks for all possible values.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The inner power takes the values $r_k=e^{-(\pi/2+2\pi k)}$. For each such real exponent, all values of the outer <complex exponentiation> are obtained from all <complex logarithm> values of its base $i$:
$$
i^{(i^i)}=\exp\left[i\left(\frac\pi2+2\pi m\right)e^{-(\pi/2+2\pi k)}\right],\qquad k,m\in\mathbb Z.
$$
Writing $\Theta_{k,m}=(\pi/2+2\pi m)e^{-(\pi/2+2\pi k)}$, the answer is
$$
\boxed{\Re(i^{(i^i)})=\cos\Theta_{k,m},\qquad\Im(i^{(i^i)})=\sin\Theta_{k,m},\qquad k,m\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
All values lie on the <unit circle>. The principal choice is $k=m=0$, giving $\cos[(\pi/2)e^{-\pi/2}]+i\sin[(\pi/2)e^{-\pi/2}]$. In particular the two nestings in parts (i) and (ii) are different: <complex exponentiation is not associative>.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <complex modulus> $|z-(-1-i)|$ is the <Euclidean distance> from $z$ to $-1-i$. Thus the locus is
$$
\boxed{(x+1)^2+(y+1)^2=64,}
$$
a <circle> with centre $(-1,-1)$ and <radius> $8$. Its extreme horizontal coordinates are $-9$ and $7$, and its extreme vertical coordinates are also $-9$ and $7$.

\Image[paper-1-q11-loci.png]
{title=The circle and its three transformed loci in Question 11, drawn with equal horizontal and vertical scales}

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $z=x+iy$ and use the <complex conjugate> $z^*=x-iy$. Then
$$
u=\frac32(x+iy)+\frac12(x-iy)=2x+iy.
$$
Thus the <real part> is stretched by a factor of two while the <imaginary part> is unchanged. If $u=X+iY$, substitution into the <circle> equation gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{(X+2)^2}{16^2}+\frac{(Y+1)^2}{8^2}=1.}
$$
This is an <ellipse> with centre $(-2,-1)$, horizontal semiaxis $16$ and vertical semiaxis $8$. It is the second panel in the figure in part (b).

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Adding $4+3i$ is a <translation> by $(4,3)$ in the <complex plane>. The <ellipse> of part (i) therefore keeps its semiaxes and axis directions, with new centre $(2,2)$. Writing $v=X+iY$, its equation is
$$
\boxed{\frac{(X-2)^2}{16^2}+\frac{(Y-2)^2}{8^2}=1.}
$$
The third panel in the figure in part (b) shows the translated <ellipse>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Multiplication by $i$ is a counterclockwise <rotation> through $\pi/2$: it maps coordinates $(X,Y)$ to $(-Y,X)$. The centre $(2,2)$ therefore becomes $(-2,2)$, and the long axis becomes vertical. For $w=X+iY$, the resulting <ellipse> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{(X+2)^2}{8^2}+\frac{(Y-2)^2}{16^2}=1.}
$$
Its horizontal semiaxis is $8$ and its vertical semiaxis is $16$, as shown in the final panel of the figure in part (b).

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

By <De Moivre's theorem>, $\sin5\theta$ is the <imaginary part> of $(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^5$. The <binomial theorem> gives, with $s=\sin\theta$,
$$
\sin5\theta=5\cos^4\theta\,s-10\cos^2\theta\,s^3+s^5.
$$
Use $\cos^2\theta=1-s^2$ and simplify:
$$
\boxed{\sin5\theta=16s^5-20s^3+5s.}
$$
The requested equation cancels the $16s^5$ term, leaving $5s(1-4s^2)=0$. Thus $s=0$ or $s=\pm1/2$. The complete list in $0\leq\theta<2\pi$ is
$$
\boxed{\theta=0,\ \frac\pi6,\ \frac{5\pi}6,\ \pi,\ \frac{7\pi}6,\ \frac{11\pi}6.}
$$

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
