= Solution
{parent=i}

The stated $x$ bounds depend on $y$, so $y$ must be held fixed while the <integral> over $x$ is performed. The intended <iterated integral> is
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1\left(\int_{2y}^2x^2y^2\,dx\right)dy.}
$$
The original PDF places the two sets of bounds beside the opposite integral signs relative to the printed $dx\,dy$. That placement cannot be read literally as a conventional nested <integral>; the dependence of the lower $x$ bound on $y$, and the explicit instruction to integrate over $x$ first, specify the intended region unambiguously.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The inequalities describing the region are $0\leq y\leq1$ and $2y\leq x\leq2$. It is the closed <triangle> bounded by $y=0$, $x=2$ and $x=2y$, with vertices
$$
\boxed{(0,0),\qquad(2,0),\qquad(2,1).}
$$
A horizontal slice runs from the sloping edge $x=2y$ to the vertical edge $x=2$.

\Image[paper-1-q12-region-and-cup.png]
{title=The triangular integration region and the cup cross-section, including its solid base, in Question 12}

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

With $y$ held fixed, the inner <antiderivative> gives
$$
\int_{2y}^2x^2y^2\,dx=\frac{y^2}{3}[x^3]_{2y}^2=\frac83(y^2-y^5).
$$
The remaining <integral> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac83\int_0^1(y^2-y^5)\,dy=\frac83\left(\frac13-\frac16\right)=\frac49.}
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For a vertical slice through the <triangle>, $0\leq x\leq2$ and $0\leq y\leq x/2$. Reversing the order by <Fubini's theorem> gives
$$
\int_0^2\left(\int_0^{x/2}x^2y^2\,dy\right)dx=\int_0^2\frac{x^5}{24}\,dx=\boxed{\frac49}.
$$
The agreement checks that both orders describe the same region.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The PDF diagram includes a solid base of thickness $d$, occupying $-d\leq z\leq0$ and $0\leq r\leq R$. Above it, $0\leq z\leq h$, the metal lies between the two specified curved surfaces. In <cylindrical coordinates>, the <Jacobian determinant> contributes a factor $r$, so the <mass> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
M={}&\rho\int_{-d}^0\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^Rr\,dr\,d\phi\,dz\\
&+\rho\int_0^h\int_0^{2\pi}\int_{(9/10)R(1+z^2/h^2)}^{R(1+z^2/h^2)}r\,dr\,d\phi\,dz.
\end{aligned}
$$
The base contributes $\pi\rho R^2d$. The wall contributes
$$
\pi\rho R^2\left(1-\frac{81}{100}\right)\int_0^h\left(1+\frac{z^2}{h^2}\right)^2dz=\pi\rho R^2\frac{19}{100}h\left(1+\frac23+\frac15\right)=\frac{133}{375}\pi\rho R^2h.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{M=\pi\rho R^2\left(d+\frac{133h}{375}\right).}
$$
The base thickness $d$ comes from the diagram; omitting it would omit part of the cup's <volume>.

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Because the original <differential equation> divides by $xy$, its domain requires $x\ne0$ and $y\ne0$. Set $u=y^2$, so $u'=2yy'$. The equation becomes the <first-order linear differential equation>
$$
u'+\frac2x u=-4x-2.
$$
An <integrating factor> is $x^2$, since $(x^2u)'=x^2u'+2xu$. Thus
$$
(x^2u)'=-4x^3-2x^2,\qquad x^2u=-x^4-\frac23x^3+C.
$$
Returning to $y$ gives the explicit general real solutions
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\pm\sqrt{\frac C{x^2}-x^2-\frac23x},\qquad C\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Each sign is fixed on a connected <interval> where $x\ne0$ and the radicand is strictly positive. The strict condition excludes $y=0$, where the original <differential equation> is undefined. There is no additional zero solution.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Where $\sin x\ne0$, divide by it to obtain $y'+2\cot x\,y=\csc x$. The <integrating factor> is $\sin^2x$, and the <product rule> gives
$$
(y\sin^2x)'=\sin x,\qquad y\sin^2x=-\cos x+C.
$$
The <initial condition> gives $C=1$. Hence
$$
y=\frac{1-\cos x}{\sin^2x}=\boxed{\frac1{1+\cos x}}.
$$
The simplified expression has a differentiable extension through $x=0$, with $y(0)=1/2$. Direct substitution verifies the original undivided <differential equation> there as well: its left side is $2y(0)=1$. Thus the maximal <interval> containing $\pi/2$ on which this solution is finite is $\boxed{-\pi<x<\pi}$; the apparent exclusion of $0$ arose only from division by $\sin x$. This is <continuation through a zero of the leading ODE coefficient>.

= 14
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An <exact differential form> must have $P_y=Q_x$. Direct <partial differentiation> gives
$$
P_y=2y\sin(ax)+2xy\cos(ax),\qquad Q_x=2y\sin(ax)+2axy\cos(ax).
$$
Thus $(1-a)xy\cos(ax)=0$ must hold identically. Since $xy\cos(ax)$ is not identically zero for a fixed nonzero $a$, the only possibility is
$$
\boxed{a=1.}
$$
The explicit potential in part (ii) verifies sufficiency.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $a=1$, integrate $Q=2xy\sin x$ with respect to $y$ to obtain
$$
f(x,y)=xy^2\sin x+C(x).
$$
Its other <partial derivative> is $f_x=y^2\sin x+xy^2\cos x+C'(x)$. Comparing with $P$ gives $C'(x)=0$. Thus the most general potential on the plane is
$$
\boxed{f(x,y)=xy^2\sin x+C,\qquad C\in\mathbb R.}
$$

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <chain rule> gives $f_x=f_u u_x+f_v v_x$ and $f_y=f_u u_y+f_v v_y$. Since $u_x=2x$, $u_y=2y$, $v_x=2xy^2$ and $v_y=2x^2y$, the answer is
$$
\boxed{f_x=2xf_u+2xy^2f_v,\qquad f_y=2yf_u+2x^2yf_v.}
$$
Every <partial derivative> of $f$ on the right is evaluated at $u=1+x^2+y^2$, $v=1+x^2y^2$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply the <product rule> and the <chain rule> once more to each expression in part (i). The <second derivative chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{f_{xx}=2f_u+2y^2f_v+4x^2f_{uu}+8x^2y^2f_{uv}+4x^2y^4f_{vv},}
$$
$$
\boxed{f_{yy}=2f_u+2x^2f_v+4y^2f_{uu}+8x^2y^2f_{uv}+4x^4y^2f_{vv},}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{f_{xy}=4xyf_{uu}+4xy(x^2+y^2)f_{uv}+4x^3y^3f_{vv}+4xyf_v.}
$$
For example, the coefficient of $f_{uv}$ in the mixed <partial derivative> is $u_xv_y+v_xu_y=4x^3y+4xy^3$, while the final term is $v_{xy}f_v=4xyf_v$. The symmetry $f_{uv}=f_{vu}$ is used in combining the mixed terms under the usual twice-continuously-differentiable interpretation.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $f(u,v)=\ln u+\ln v$, the needed <partial derivatives> are
$$
f_v=\frac1v,\qquad f_{uu}=-\frac1{u^2},\qquad f_{uv}=0,\qquad f_{vv}=-\frac1{v^2}.
$$
Substituting these into part (ii) gives
$$
f_{xy}=-\frac{4xy}{u^2}-\frac{4x^3y^3}{v^2}+\frac{4xy}{v}.
$$
Since $v-x^2y^2=1$, the last two terms combine. Thus
$$
\boxed{f_{xy}=4xy\left[\frac1{(1+x^2y^2)^2}-\frac1{(1+x^2+y^2)^2}\right].}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

First express the <function> in the original variables:
$$
f(x,y)=\ln(1+x^2+y^2)+\ln(1+x^2y^2).
$$
The <chain rule> gives
$$
f_x=\frac{2x}{1+x^2+y^2}+\frac{2xy^2}{1+x^2y^2}.
$$
Taking its <partial derivative> with respect to $y$ yields
$$
f_{xy}=-\frac{4xy}{(1+x^2+y^2)^2}+\frac{4xy(1+x^2y^2)-4x^3y^3}{(1+x^2y^2)^2}.
$$
The numerator in the second fraction reduces to $4xy$, reproducing
$$
\boxed{f_{xy}=4xy\left[\frac1{(1+x^2y^2)^2}-\frac1{(1+x^2+y^2)^2}\right].}
$$
Both <logarithms> are defined for every real $x,y$ because their arguments are at least one.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $a\ne0$, factor out $|a|$, not $a$, and apply the <binomial series> with exponent $1/2$:
$$
\sqrt{a^2+x^2}=|a|\left(1+\frac{x^2}{a^2}\right)^{1/2}=\boxed{|a|+\frac{x^2}{2|a|}-\frac{x^4}{8|a|^3}+O(x^6).}
$$
For each fixed nonzero $a$, these are the first three nonzero terms of the <Taylor series> about zero; the full <binomial series> converges when $|x|<|a|$.

For $a=0$, the <function> is $|x|$. Its right <derivative> at zero is $1$ and its left <derivative> is $-1$, so **no Taylor series about zero exists when $a=0$**. The one-sided formulas are exactly $x$ for $x\geq0$ and $-x$ for $x\leq0$; they do not form a single two-sided <Taylor expansion>, nor can they supply three nonzero terms. The parameter case explicitly included in the question must therefore be treated as this exception.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $t_j=t^{(j)}(0)$ and $s=1+t_0>0$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
f(0)=\ln s,\qquad f'(0)=\frac{t_1}{s}.
$$
The condition $t_3t_1<0$ ensures $t_1\ne0$. Since $t_0\ne0$, also $s\ne1$ and $\ln s\ne0$. Hence the first two nonzero terms are the constant and linear terms:
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\ln(1+t_0)+\frac{t_1}{1+t_0}x+O(x^2).}
$$
The inequality $s>0$, together with <continuity> of $t$, makes the <logarithm> defined on a neighbourhood of zero. The remainder is written in <Big O notation>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <second derivative chain rule> gives
$$
f''(x)=\frac{t''(x)}{1+t(x)}-\frac{t'(x)^2}{(1+t(x))^2}.
$$
With $t_0=0$, the constant term vanishes, $f'(0)=t_1\ne0$, and $f''(0)=t_2-t_1^2\ne0$ by the specified case. Thus the first two nonzero terms in the <Taylor expansion> are
$$
\boxed{f(x)=t_1x+\frac{t_2-t_1^2}{2}x^2+O(x^3).}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Here $t_0=0$ and $t_2=t_1^2$, so the constant and quadratic terms vanish, while the linear coefficient remains $t_1\ne0$. A further <derivative> gives
$$
f^{(3)}(x)=\frac{t^{(3)}(x)}{1+t(x)}-\frac{3t'(x)t''(x)}{(1+t(x))^2}+\frac{2t'(x)^3}{(1+t(x))^3}.
$$
Consequently $f^{(3)}(0)=t_3-3t_1t_2+2t_1^3=t_3-t_1^3$. Since $t_3$ and $t_1$ have opposite signs, $t_3$ and $t_1^3$ also have opposite signs, and their difference is nonzero. The first two nonzero terms are therefore
$$
\boxed{f(x)=t_1x+\frac{t_3-t_1^3}{6}x^3+O(x^4).}
$$
This is where the full sign condition $t_3t_1<0$ rules out a further cancellation in the <Taylor expansion>.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
