= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <probability density function> must have total <integral> one. The numerator is an <even function>, so
$$
N=2\int_0^2(1+e^{-x})\,dx=2[\,x-e^{-x}\,]_0^2=\boxed{6-2e^{-2}}.
$$
This is positive, ensuring nonnegativity of the <probability density function>.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $N=6-2e^{-2}$. The <function graph> is symmetric about the vertical axis, zero outside $[-2,2]$, and has its unique maximum at $x=0$ with height $2/N$. On $0<x<2$, it decreases as $(1+e^{-x})/N$; the negative side is its reflection. The one-sided interior endpoint values are $(1+e^{-2})/N$, so the <probability density function> jumps to zero at both ends of its support. It also has a corner at its central maximum because of the <absolute value> in the exponent.

\Image[paper-1-q16-density.png]
{title=The symmetric density in Question 16, including its central corner and endpoint jumps}

Values assigned exactly at $x=\pm2$ do not affect any <probability>.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Since the <probability density function> is an <even function>, $xf(x)$ is an <odd function>. Its <integral> over the symmetric support vanishes, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X]=0.}
$$

= d
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <expected value> is zero, so the <variance> is $\mathbb E[X^2]$. Symmetry gives
$$
\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac2N\int_0^2x^2(1+e^{-x})\,dx.
$$
The polynomial term contributes $8/3$. Two applications of <integration by parts> give
$$
\int x^2e^{-x}\,dx=-(x^2+2x+2)e^{-x}+C,
$$
so its definite value on $[0,2]$ is $2-10e^{-2}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{28/3-20e^{-2}}{6-2e^{-2}}.}
$$

= e
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Let $F$ be the <cumulative distribution function> of $X$. Normalisation of the proposed <probability density function> requires $M=\int_{-2}^2F(y)\,dy$. The <integrated cumulative distribution function on a bounded interval> identity gives
$$
M=2-\mathbb E[X]=\boxed{2}.
$$
For a direct symmetry proof, the continuous symmetric <probability distribution> satisfies $F(-y)=1-F(y)$. Hence
$$
\int_{-2}^2F(y)\,dy=\int_0^2[F(y)+F(-y)]\,dy=\int_0^2 1\,dy=2.
$$
In particular $g(y)=F(y)/2$ is nonnegative and has total <integral> one over its stated support.

