Write $I=\int e^x\sin x\,dx$ and $J=\int e^x\cos x\,dx$. Two applications of <integration by parts>, first taking the <sine> and then the <cosine> as the differentiated factor, give
$$
\boxed{I=e^x\sin x-J+C_1,\qquad J=e^x\cos x+I+C_2.}
$$
Thus $I+J=e^x\sin x$ and $J-I=e^x\cos x$, in each case up to an arbitrary constant of <integration>. Keeping that freedom avoids identifying independently chosen <antiderivatives> too rigidly.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The difference and sum of the two <antiderivatives> in part (i) give
$$
\boxed{\int e^x(\sin x-\cos x)\,dx=-e^x\cos x+C,}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{\int e^x(\sin x+\cos x)\,dx=e^x\sin x+C.}
$$
Their <derivatives> also verify the signs immediately.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $I_n=\int e^{nx}\sin x\,dx$ and $J_n=\int e^{nx}\cos x\,dx$. Integrating the <trigonometric functions> and differentiating $e^{nx}$ in <integration by parts> gives, modulo constants,
$$
I_n=-e^{nx}\cos x+nJ_n,\qquad J_n=e^{nx}\sin x-nI_n.
$$
Substitution yields $(1+n^2)I_n=e^{nx}(n\sin x-\cos x)$ and $(1+n^2)J_n=e^{nx}(n\cos x+\sin x)$. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int e^{nx}(\sin x-\cos x)\,dx&=I_n-J_n\\
&=\boxed{-\frac{e^{nx}}{n^2+1}\left[(1+n)\cos x+(1-n)\sin x\right]+C.}
\end{aligned}
$$
No division by $n$ was used, so this formula also applies at $n=0$.

