= Solution
{parent=a}

Adding the diagonal entries gives $\operatorname{tr}A=4+4+2=10$. Expansion along the first row gives $\det A=4(8-4)+2(0-8)=0$. For the remaining <trace>, direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
A^2=\begin{pmatrix}20&4&12\\4&20&12\\12&12&12\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Thus $\operatorname{tr}(A^2)=20+20+12=52$. By <multiplicativity of the determinant>, $\det(A^2)=(\det A)^2=0$. Altogether,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}A=10,\qquad\det A=0,\qquad\operatorname{tr}(A^2)=52,\qquad\det(A^2)=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <vector> $(1,-1,0)^T$ is an <eigenvector> with <eigenvalue> $4$. The complementary subspace of <vectors> with first two components equal is preserved by $A$: a vector $(s,s,t)^T$ maps to $(4s+2t,4s+2t,4s+2t)^T$. There, the <eigenvalue> equations reduce to the $2\times2$ <matrix> $\begin{pmatrix}4&2\\4&2\end{pmatrix}$, whose <characteristic polynomial> is $\lambda(\lambda-6)$. For $\lambda=6$, $t=s$; for $\lambda=0$, $t=-2s$.

Normalising by the <Euclidean norm>, a complete <orthonormal basis> of <eigenvectors> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\lambda&\text{unit eigenvector}\\\hline
0&\mathbf e_0=(1,1,-2)^T/\sqrt6\\
4&\mathbf e_4=(1,-1,0)^T/\sqrt2\\
6&\mathbf e_6=(1,1,1)^T/\sqrt3
\end{array}}
$$
Sign changes of any of these <eigenvectors> are equally valid. The <eigenvalues> also check part (a): their sum is $10$ and the sum of their squares is $52$.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Expand the right-hand side in the <orthonormal basis> from part (b). Its coefficients are
$$
\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf e_0=0,\qquad\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf e_4=2\sqrt2,\qquad\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf e_6=\sqrt3.
$$
Thus $\mathbf b=2\sqrt2\mathbf e_4+\sqrt3\mathbf e_6$. If $\mathbf x=c_0\mathbf e_0+c_4\mathbf e_4+c_6\mathbf e_6$, then <matrix multiplication> gives $A\mathbf x=4c_4\mathbf e_4+6c_6\mathbf e_6$. Matching coefficients fixes $c_4=\sqrt2/2$ and $c_6=\sqrt3/6$, while $c_0$ is arbitrary. Consequently all solutions are
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x=\begin{pmatrix}2/3\\-1/3\\1/6\end{pmatrix}+t\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\\-2\end{pmatrix},\qquad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Geometrically, the solution set is an <affine line in a vector space> parallel to the <kernel> of $A$. The zero <eigenvalue> makes the component in that direction invisible to $A$; the other two components are uniquely determined. The displayed particular solution is perpendicular to the <kernel>, so it is also the point of this <line> closest to the origin.

