For every integer $n\geq1$, $\sin((2n-1)\pi)=0$ and $\cos((2n-3)\pi)=-1$. Thus the summand simplifies to $2/n$. The resulting <series> is twice the <harmonic series>, so **it diverges to $+\infty$**.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The inner <geometric series> has common ratio $2/3$, so
$$
\sum_{m=n}^{\infty}\frac{2^m}{3^m}=\frac{(2/3)^n}{1-2/3}=3(2/3)^n.
$$
The outer <geometric series> therefore converges as well, with value
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\sum_{m=n}^{\infty}(2/3)^m=3\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(2/3)^n=9.}
$$
All summands are nonnegative, so there is no cancellation or conditional-convergence issue in evaluating the nested sums.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The summands repeat the six-term pattern
$$
\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\quad\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\quad0,\quad-\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\quad-\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\quad0.
$$
In particular they do not tend to zero: the subsequence with $n=6k+1$ is always $\sqrt3/2$. The <term test for divergence> therefore shows that **the <series> diverges**. Although each block of six sums to zero, that only makes a subsequence of <partial sums> zero; it does not make the full sequence of <partial sums> convergent.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the positive terms $u_n=2^n/n!$, the consecutive ratio is $u_{n+1}/u_n=2/(n+1)\to0$, so the <ratio test> proves convergence. The <exponential series> gives $e^2=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}2^n/n!$. Removing the $n=0$ term yields
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{2^n}{n!}=e^2-1.}
$$

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $S_N=\sum_{n=1}^N(-1)^{n+1}/n$. Integrate the finite <geometric series> identity $\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(-t)^k=[1-(-t)^N]/(1+t)$ over $0\leq t\leq1$:
$$
S_N=\int_0^1\frac{1-(-t)^N}{1+t}\,dt=\ln2-(-1)^N\int_0^1\frac{t^N}{1+t}\,dt.
$$
The remainder has <absolute value> at most $\int_0^1t^Ndt=1/(N+1)\to0$. Hence the <partial sums> converge, with
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n=\ln2.}
$$
This proves both convergence and the value of the <alternating harmonic series> without substituting an endpoint into an unproved infinite <power series>. Its convergence is conditional because the corresponding <harmonic series> of <absolute values> diverges.

